1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:02,680
Imagine paying two thousand rupees online.

2
00:00:02,720 --> 00:00:04,560
You click Pay.

3
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:06,400
The screen freezes.

4
00:00:06,440 --> 00:00:09,600
So you click again. Did you just pay twice?

5
00:00:09,680 --> 00:00:12,860
Both requests might reach the payment server.

6
00:00:12,900 --> 00:00:17,460
Without protection, the system could process the same payment twice.

7
00:00:17,580 --> 00:00:21,140
That's why payment systems use idempotency keys.

8
00:00:21,180 --> 00:00:26,760
The client attaches the same unique key to every retry of one payment operation.

9
00:00:26,920 --> 00:00:31,200
The first request processes the payment and stores its result.

10
00:00:31,240 --> 00:00:37,260
When the retry arrives, the server finds the same key and returns the saved result instead of charging again.

11
00:00:37,420 --> 00:00:40,700
But what if both requests arrive simultaneously?

12
00:00:40,740 --> 00:00:46,220
Atomic key reservation ensures only one request can own the operation.

13
00:00:46,400 --> 00:00:48,820
That's payment idempotency.

14
00:00:48,860 --> 00:00:53,000
One payment operation, even when clients retry.

15
00:00:53,040 --> 00:00:56,440
A small key that solves a big distributed systems problem.
